※ pl4g-test: compile=ok run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ The tolerance is a variable and not a number built into the compiler,
※ because the right one depends on how far the values being compared have
※ travelled, which is the program's business.  `⎕tolerance` is the name it is
※ read and written by; a name beginning with `⎕` is the compiler's, so no
※ program can have had that name taken away from it.

let one_value: mut f64 = 1f64
let other: mut f64 = 1.5f64

let zero: mut u8 = 0u8
let one: u8 = 1u8

fn stop_unless(ok: bool):
    @[ignore(5005)]
    ok or (one ÷ zero = one)

@[startup]
fn main() → u8:
    ※ half apart, which the tolerance it starts with does not allow for
    stop_unless(one_value ≇ other)
    ⎕tolerance ← 1f64
    ※ and does once the program says how much error it is willing to see
    stop_unless(one_value ≅ other)
    0u8
